{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-60","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"60","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the National Park Service relating to \"Glen Canyon National Recreation Area: Motor Vehicles\".","introducedDate":"2025-02-21","latestActionDate":"2025-05-23","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-13.","policyArea":"Public Lands and Natural Resources","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Arizona","Department of the Interior","Land use and conservation","Motor vehicles","Parks, recreation areas, trails","Utah"],"sponsorBioguideId":"M001228","cosponsorBioguideIds":["K000403","M001213","O000086"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution allows off-road vehicles to be used in certain areas of the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, which is in northern Arizona and southeastern Utah and contains Lake Powell. Specifically, the joint resolution nullifies the rule issued by the National Park Service (NPS) titled Glen Canyon National Recreation Area; Motor Vehicles and published on January 13, 2025. Under the rule, the NPS limited the use of off-road vehicles, such as off-highway vehicles and all-terrain vehicles, in certain areas. For instance, the rule prohibited the use of off-road vehicles on an 8-mile segment of the Poison Spring Loop located on Route 633 proceeding north to Route 730 in the Orange Cliffs Special Management Unit. The rule also eliminated the authority of the NPS to open the upper portion of the Flint Trail in that unit to off-road vehicles. Additionally, the rule limited off-road vehicle use from certain roads to the shoreline of the lake.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/60","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres60.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:16.941Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-60","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would overturn a National Park Service rule that restricted off-road vehicle use in parts of Glen Canyon National Recreation Area in Arizona and Utah. The NPS rule, published in January 2025, prohibited off-road vehicles on certain trails and roads, including an 8-mile segment of the Poison Spring Loop and routes leading to Lake Powell's shoreline. If passed, the resolution would nullify these restrictions and allow off-road vehicle access to continue in those areas.","industries":[{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Off-road vehicle access affects tourism and recreation businesses serving visitors to Glen Canyon National Recreation Area."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Off-road vehicle manufacturers and dealers have a direct stake in regulations that expand or restrict where their products can be used."},{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Retailers selling off-road vehicles and related equipment are affected by rules governing where these products can be operated."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"aea3ed2228d54798ee2fc794","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:45:46.746Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would allow off-road vehicles to be used in certain areas of the Glen Canyon National Recreation Area, which is in northern Arizona and southeastern Utah and contains Lake Powell.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People who visit public land, and people who live beside it.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in driving, and what a car must have. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hj